    "_market_evidence": "Coffee powder retail average rose 43.08 -> 45.22 over six days while we sold 100 units into it; saturation 1.295 and demand 0.352 versus apples at 1.490 / 0.255. The market absorbs it.",
    "_after_mill": "Build order after the Mill, by capital efficiency and durability: (1) Bakery $42k — dough $1,304/h, 32h payback, best in the game; (2) Oil rig $76k — crude oil $1,649/h at 72.6% margin, the most durable demand there is (fuel feeds everything); slots then full at Lv.5, so (3) upgrade Oil rig L2, then Bakery/Mill L2; at Lv.10 the 6th slot goes to a Construction factory (tools/planks/windows, all $1,100-1,500/h). Catering is excluded from this plan by owner decision 2026-07-21.",
    "_working_capital": "Raised from 31k after Jimmy caught the gap: build (~31.4k) + farm bean-order wages (520) + mill wages prepaid (1,900) + seed/water top-up (~3,650) + reserve (500) ≈ 38k. A building with no float sits idle at $380/h in wages.",
    "_startup_sequence": "On Mill completion: (1) farm switches from APPLES to COFFEE BEANS immediately — beans need 1-2h to accumulate; (2) mill starts its first powder order only when warehouse beans >= 10x the order size; grocery keeps selling remaining apples, then powder.",
    "_reorder": "FINAL 2026-07-21 23:35: Mill first after all. The -23% powder nerf (until 7/27) cuts it to $700/h, but its $38k gate arrives ~20h before the Bakery's $47k — and $700/h while saving beats $0/h waiting. Queue: Mill -> Bakery -> Oil rig. Farm L2 (accidental but useful) can alternate bean batches for the mill and apple batches for the store.",
    "_after": "STANDING QUEUE (re-confirmed by strategist 2026-07-22 12:30, fresh re-rank): Mill NOW ($34.5k gate) -> Bakery -> Oil rig -> level upgrades -> Construction factory at Lv.10. The 23:30 Bakery-first reorder was reversed at 23:35 (Mill's gate arrives ~20h earlier; earning during the nerf beats waiting). Slaughterhouse (steak $994/h, 23h payback) checked 7/22 and rejected: cattle float ~$8.7k/5h order pushes its true gate to ~Mill's, with market-input exposure the Mill doesn't have. Still to evaluate once slots allow: Food processing plant ($94k, apple icecream in season until 8/20, then cheese/butter/chocolate; not Catering).",
    "_gate_refit": "2026-07-22 17:10 CDT: gate 34.5k -> 19k. The Mill dialog was probed directly: catalogue quote $30,548, but the warehouse already holds 24/32 reinforced concrete, 330/440 bricks, 96/128 planks and 3/8 construction units (the PA's free gift) — $14.8k of it. Missing materials priced at live asks: 8 concrete @204 + 110 bricks @2.75 + 32 planks @10.2 + 5 construction units @2670 = $15,611. Gate = missing 15.6k + mill wage float 1.9k + farm wage 0.5k + reserve 0.5k + ~0.5k ask-slippage slack = 19k. effectiveCost (16.2k) is passed to build.js so its affordability check uses the real cash outlay, not the full quote. Prior note (00:20): the 10k water stock assumed then has since been consumed by grape orders; bean startup water rebuys at ~$190/h, immaterial. 2026-07-22 18:55 CDT: gate 19k -> 18.5k. The 1.9k mill wage float double-counts: it is not needed until the mill completes ~3h after the build fires, and the store adds ~$2.7k of revenue in that window. Cut so the strategist could supervise build.js's first live run instead of letting it fire unattended at the next heartbeat. 2026-07-22 19:05 CDT CORRECTION, gate -> 24.8k: the supervised run exposed that the warehouse holds ZERO construction units — the PA's 3 free CUs were consumed by the store L2 upgrade on 7/21 (its $8,030 materials offset = 3 x $2,670 + change). The 17:10 probe read '3 CUs owned' out of the PA chat history, not the dialog, and its 15,611+14,793=quote cross-check was an identity (the split always sums to the quote) so it could not catch the error. Live dialog: BUY MISSING $23,450 = 8 concrete@204 + 110 bricks@2.70 + 32 planks@10.1 + 8 CU@2,650. Gate = 23.45k + 0.5k reserve + 0.55k wage/slippage headroom + ~0.3k farm float. CU book is deep (4,778 @ 2,650) — no rush-buy needed. build.js now reads the live missing cost off the BUY MISSING button and gates on that, so a stale effectiveCost can never overspend. 2026-07-23 00:05 CDT: gate 24.8k -> 24.2k, effectiveCost -> 23,708 (bill repriced off the 23:49 ticker: 8 RC@206 + 110 bricks@2.70 + 32 planks@10.1 + 8 CU@2,680; CU is_up, +$30 since 19:05). The 24.8k figure was a price snapshot, not a policy: build.js's own two safety gates (effectiveCost pre-check, then the live BUY MISSING price vs minCashAfter) mean the config gate only ever DELAYS the build, never overspends it — endorsed by CFO/COO in the 7-22 special session (\"gate should track the remaining bill, $24,800 is a stale snapshot\"). Net effect: fires ~$600 of cash earlier ≈ 1h sooner at the ~$650/h store inflow; if CU drifts up further, the pre-check bails cheaply each heartbeat until cash covers bill+500 — which is exactly the right moment.",
